<div class="section-background color-{{ section.settings.color_scheme }}"></div>
{% comment %}
  At first glance, this looks like normal HTML.
  But this part:
  {{ section.settings.color_scheme }}
    
   section.settings is 'the settings configured for this section in the Theme Editor.

   section.settings.product
   means 'the product selected in the Theme Editor.'

   section.settings.color_scheme
   means 'the color scheme selected in the Theme Editor.'

   why do we have hyphen -        section-background color-{{ section.settings.color_scheme }}
   Suppose the merchant chooses:   scheme-1

   Shopify might produce:  class="section-background color-scheme-1"
   So in a nutshell    Liquid is dynamically constructing the CSS class.
   as

   "color-" + whatever the merchant selected




   As you can see the div is empty... nothing is inside
   It's probably being used as a background layer.
   For example, CSS could say:

  .section-background {
  position: absolute;
  inset: 0;
}

so the color class determines its background color.

{% endcomment %}



<div class="featured-product-section section section--full-width color-{{ section.settings.color_scheme }}">
    {% comment %}
     This is the main container.

     There are four classes here

     Also 
     color-{{ section.settings.color_scheme }}
     Again, dynamically applies the selected color scheme.

    {% endcomment %}



<div class="section-content-wrapper ...">   
    {% comment %}
      This is basically the container holding the actual content.

    
    {% endcomment %}







 {% capture featured_product_media %}

    {% comment %}

    capture means:

    Run the Liquid inside here and store the resulting HTML in a variable called
    'featured_product_media'

      
    {% endcomment %}

    Inside the capture... we have 

    {% content_for 'block',
    type: '_media-without-appearance',
    id: 'media',
    closest.product: section.settings.product
    %}



    {% comment %}
      {% content_for 'block' %}       This tells Shopify:  "Render a block here."


      type: '_media-without-appearance'       This tells Shopify which block type to render.

      Note;;;;;;           The _ is significant in Shopify's newer theme architecture: it commonly indicates a private/internal theme block, 
                           rather than a block intended to be independently added by merchants in the Theme Editor.
                           So this block probably handles the product images/media.



    id: 'media'                   This gives that block an ID:




    closest.product: section.settings.product

    This is saying:

    "For this block, treat the product selected in this section's settings as the closest product."


    Remember;   section.settings.product  means       The product the merchant selected in the Theme Editor.




    {% endcomment %}







    {% capture featured_product_content %}


    {% comment %}
      This alos mean
      Create a variable called featured_product_content and 
      store some generated HTML inside it.
    {% endcomment %}

    {% content_for 'block',
  type: '_featured-product',
  id: 'featured-product',
  closest.product: section.settings.product
  %}

  featured_product_media     probably mean: Product images    or     Product videos    or   Media gallery 
  
  featured_product_content   probably mean: Product title    or     Price    or   Variant picker  or  Description or  Add to cart  etc






