Now comes the decision

{% if section.settings.layout == 'media-left' %}

means:
"If the layout setting is equal to media-left..."

section.settings.layout          
also means: 
The layout selected in the Theme Editor.

Maybe the merchant has:
Layout:
○ Media left
○ Content left



If it's media-left...
so the media will be at the left and the content will be at the right
{% else %}
 means: "If the condition wasn't true, do this instead."
 
 

 Also notice what capture does

 Normally:
 {% content_for 'block', ... %}
would render something.



But:
{% capture something %}
    ...generate something...
{% endcapture %}

means:  Generate it, but put the result into a variable first.


So:

{% capture featured_product_media %}
    ...
{% endcapture %}

creates:  featured_product_media = generated HTML
Then later:        {{ featured_product_media }}








Why didn't they just render the blocks directly? without using Capture like the below

{% if section.settings.layout == 'media-left' %}

    {% content_for 'block', type: '_media-without-appearance' %}
    {% content_for 'block', type: '_featured-product' %}

{% else %}

    {% content_for 'block', type: '_featured-product' %}
    {% content_for 'block', type: '_media-without-appearance' %}

{% endif %}


They could structure it that way in some implementations.

But capture allows the developer to prepare/store each piece first, then output the pieces wherever they want.

That's particularly useful when the generated content is more complicated.





As you can see, this file is not self-contained. It's a "section" file that just orchestrates other 
files it doesn't include. Here's what's missing from what we have: 
almost certainly living somewhere like /blocks/ or /snippets/ in the theme.


1. _media-without-appearance — a separate block file that renders the actual image/gallery
2. _featured-product — a separate block file that itself likely composes more child blocks 
(you can see this in the schema's presets:
     product-title, _featured-product-price, _featured-product-gallery, swatches)
3. (product-title, _featured-product-price, _featured-product-gallery, swatches) 
is almost certainly its own block file too
4. Translation files
Everything like "t:names.featured_product" and "t:settings.product" pulls 
text from a locale JSON file (e.g. en.default.schema.json), not from this file.
5. structured_data filter
This is a built-in Shopify filter (not external code you need), but worth knowing it's 
generating SEO/rich-snippet JSON-LD automatically, not something defined in this file.



