Explanation
I want you to see the overall structure first:

THIS FILE
│
├── 1. Liquid logic
│      └── Decide whether image should be "unset"
│
├── 2. Render a snippet
│      └── Send information to the snippet
│
└── 3. Schema
       └── Tell Shopify Theme Editor
           what settings this block should have


Why call it           unset_image_tag 
The name is telling us what the variable is intended to control.   

roughly means:
"Should we unset/remove something about the image tag?"

{% assign unset_image_tag = false %}
means:
Start by assuming we DON'T need to unset the image tag.

{% if block.settings.image_position == 'contain' %}
 This is asking a question:
"Is the image position set to contain?"   

Remember from your schema later, you have:

{
  "value": "cover",
  "label": "..."
},
{
  "value": "contain",
  "label": "..."
}

So the merchant can choose:

Image position
○ Cover
○ Contain


If they choose contain, then:
block.settings.image_position     equals:    contain
Therefore:
{% block.settings.image_position == 'contain' %}
becomes:
contain == contain



So the logic is:

START
  ↓
unset_image_tag = false
  ↓
Is image_position "contain"?
  ↓
YES
  ↓
unset_image_tag = true


If the merchant chooses cover:

START
  ↓
unset_image_tag = false
  ↓
Is image_position "contain"?
  ↓
NO
  ↓
leave it false



So this:
{% liquid

  assign unset_image_tag = false

  if block.settings.image_position == 'contain'
    assign unset_image_tag = true
  endif

%}
basically means:
Start with unset_image_tag as false. If the image position is contain, change it to true.



This is a different Liquid command.
{% render 'media', unset_image_tag: unset_image_tag, section_id: section.id %}
means:
"Render the media snippet, and give that snippet these pieces of information."

{% unset_image_tag: unset_image_tag %}
This looks repetitive, but there's an important distinction.
The left side:     is the name the snippet will receive.
The right side:    is the variable from this current file.
So:
{% unset_image_tag: unset_image_tag %}
means:
"Inside the media snippet, create/use a variable called unset_image_tag, 
and give it the value of my current unset_image_tag variable."

For example, if:
{% current unset_image_tag = true %}

then the snippet receives:
media snippet
    ↓
unset_image_tag = true


{% section_id: section.id %}
means:
"Give the media snippet the current section's ID and call that value section_id."

If the current section's ID were:
template--123456
the snippet gets:
section_id = template--123456

Whenever you see:
{% render 'something', name: value %}
read it as:
Render something and pass it name = value.

For example:
{% render 'product-card', product: product %}
means:
product-card snippet
       ↓
product = current product



Now we reach {% schema %}
it tells Shopify:
"Here is how this block should appear and behave in the Theme Editor."

{
  "name": "t:names.media",
  "tag": null,
  "settings": [...]
}

"name": "t:names.media"
This tells Shopify the name of the block.
But You might see something like: Media         in the Theme Editor.
t:names.media       is a translation key.

Instead of hardcoding:
{
  "name": "Media",
}
the theme can reference a translation instead.

"tag": null
This is saying the block doesn't request a specific HTML wrapper tag through this schema setting.

some Shopify sections can specify:
{
  "tag": "section"
}
which can influence the wrapper.
In our case:  Null means                 No specific tag is defined here.


{
  "settings": [...]
}
This means:
"Here are all the controls the merchant gets in the Theme Editor."
Everything inside this array becomes a setting.
You have:

media_type
image
link
video
video_loop
video_autoplay
image_position
video_position





First setting: media type
{
  "type": "select",
  "id": "media_type",
  "label": "t:settings.type",
}

type       select              creates a dropdown/select control.

id      media_type             This is the internal name of the setting.
Therefore Liquid can access it using: 
block.settings.media_type   



The options
"options": [
  {
    "value": "image",
    "label": "t:options.image"
  },
  {
    "value": "video",
    "label": "t:options.video"
  }
]

The merchant gets two choices:
Image
Video


label                          What the merchant sees.


value                          What Liquid receives.

So:
Theme Editor
     ↓
Merchant chooses "Image"
     ↓
Liquid value = "image"


Then:
block.settings.media_type

returns:
image




Now the image setting
{
  "type": "image_picker",
  "id": "image",
  "label": "t:settings.image",
}
This creates Shopify's image picker.

The merchant can choose an image from Shopify.

Its internal ID is:           image
Therefore Liquid can access it with:          block.settings.image





Now we have:
"visible_if": "{{ block.settings.media_type == 'image' }}"
This is a very useful Shopify feature.
It says:
Only show this setting when the media type is image.





Without visible_if, the merchant might see:

Media type
[ Video ▼ ]

Image
[ Choose image ]

Link
[ URL ]

Video
[ Choose video ]

Loop
☑

Autoplay
☐

That's confusing.



With visible_if:

If Image:

Media type
[ Image ▼ ]

Image
[ Choose image ]

Link
[ URL ]

Image position
[ Cover ▼ ]




If Video:

Media type
[ Video ▼ ]

Video
[ Choose video ]

Loop
☑

Autoplay
☐

Video position
[ Cover ▼ ]

Much cleaner.




The link setting
{
  "type": "url",
  "id": "link",
  "label": "t:settings.link",
  "visible_if": "{{ block.settings.media_type == 'image' }}"
}
This gives the merchant a URL field.

Liquid can access it as:
{ block.settings.link }
And it only appears when:
media_type = image

This makes sense because this particular setting is apparently intended to make the image clickable.




Video setting
{
  "type": "video",
  "id": "video",
}
This gives the merchant a Shopify video picker.
And:
"visible_if": "{{ block.settings.media_type == 'video' }}"
means:
Only show the video picker when the merchant has chosen Video.




Video loop
{
  "type": "checkbox",
  "id": "video_loop",
  "label": "t:settings.loop",
  "default": true,
}
The setting is called:
video_loop

So Liquid can access:
block.settings.video_loop

"default": true
means:                          The checkbox starts turned ON.



Video autoplay
Same idea:
{
  "type": "checkbox",
  "id": "video_autoplay",
}
So Liquid can access:
block.settings.video_autoplay

"default": false

means:                           The video does NOT autoplay by default.





Now image_position
{
  "type": "select",
  "id": "image_position",
}
Another dropdown.

The merchant chooses between:
Cover
Contain

Cover   means:                  The image fills the container.
                                But parts of the image may get cropped.
                                Lke in CSS          object-fit: cover;



Contain   means:                The entire image remains visible:
                                potentially leaving space.
                                Lke in CSS          object-fit: contain;




 
                                

Finally: presets
"presets": [
  {
    "name": "t:names.media"
  }
]
A preset is essentially a starting configuration for the block
when it is added through the editor.

It tells Shopify:

"This block has a default/preset configuration called Media."                                




Then we close the schema
{% endschema %}
This tells Shopify:
"The schema configuration ends here."

Now let's connect EVERYTHING
You have a setting in the schema:
"id": "image_position"                    This then creates:                         block.settings.image_position
Then your Liquid asks:                               if block.settings.image_position == 'contain'

That's the connection between schema and Liquid.












