Explanation
The variable is:                     product_card_children
The first <div>                        It creates a container for the top part of the product card.
Inside it we have:    

featured-product-content-top
│
├── Product title
└── Product price


{% content_for 'block', type: 'product-title', id: 'featured-product-title' %}
means:                        "Render the product-title block here."
content_for 'block',                   means:      "I want to render a block.".
type: 'product-title'          means:       The type of block I want is product-title
id: 'featured-product-title'   means:       gives this particular block instance an identifier.


{% content_for 'block', type: '_featured-product-price', id: 'featured-product-price' %}
means:                        "Render the   _featured-product-price block     here.."
_featured-product-price       and          product-title         are simply different block types.

The underscore is part of the block's type/name.
It's basically part of the identifier:                    _featured-product-price



Then another <div>                                 <div class="featured-product-content-bottom">
{%- content_for 'block', type: 'swatches', id: 'featured-product-swatches' -%}
This renders the swatches or product variant choices.

● Black   ● White   ● Blue



So Shopify has now created:         product_card_children
containing all this:

<div class="featured-product-content-top">
    product title
    product price
</div>

product gallery

<div class="featured-product-content-bottom">
    swatches
</div>




