Imagine you have a big plastic storage box in your room, and 
you wrote the word closest on the outside of the box with a marker.
Inside this box, you have stuffed a bunch of different things: a toy car, a map, and a product 
(let's say it's a cool superhero toy).If you just tell your computer: 
"Hey, go find the product!" The computer will look around the room, get confused, and say, 
"Which product? The room is messy! I don't see it!"But if you say closest.product, 
you are telling the computer exactly where to look:"Go to the box labeled closest...""...reach inside, 
and grab the product that is hidden in there."Why can't we just say product = product?If you tell your friend, 
"Give me the toy using the toy," your friend will think you are being silly! 
The computer thinks the same way. It needs you to tell it where to get the toy from. 
Saying product = closest.product is just your way of saying: 
"Take the toy out of the closest box, and hand it to me."


